# Conventional Commits

Every commit message **must** start with a Conventional Commits type prefix.

## Format

```
<type>: <short summary in English>
```

Optional scope: `feat(fathom): …` / `fix(install): …`

## Types

| Type | Use when |
|------|----------|
| `feat` | New capability or user-facing addition |
| `fix` | Bug fix |
| `chore` | Maintenance (deps, hooks, tooling, cleanup with no behavior change) |
| `docs` | Documentation only |
| `refactor` | Code change with no feature and no bug fix |
| `test` | Adding or updating tests |
| `style` | Formatting only (no logic change) |
| `perf` | Performance improvement |
| `ci` | CI / GitHub Actions / pipeline config |

## Do

- Prefer the **why** in the summary (not a file dump)
- Keep the first line ≤ ~72 characters
- Use imperative mood: `add`, `fix`, `update` (not `added` / `fixes`)

## Don't

- Don't commit without a type prefix
- Don't use vague messages like `update`, `wip`, `fix stuff`
- Don't put the whole changelog in the subject — use the body if needed

## Examples

```
# ❌ BAD
update fathom skill
WIP
fixed bug

# ✅ GOOD
feat(fathom): add video download via ffmpeg
fix(install): keep shared venv when pip init fails
chore: rename remaining ai-skills paths to meta-skills
docs: clarify CURRENT_SKILL_DIRECTORY in fathom SKILL.md
```
